Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 21:47:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10332/11362] Scope the actions-to-progress A/B for the tool
 loop, and say why not the control arm
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The holdout A/B's control arm costs days (~4,238s/cell against the tool arm's
~598s) and buys a paired result on a metric the operator has said is not the one
that matters: it scores holdout generalization and records NO level progress at
all, while a memorizing engine that banks levels is doing its job at run time.
Finishing a proxy before running the real measurement is the wrong order.

The harness already exists (REQ-ARC-WMTE-5720) and five experiments drive it,
including a tool-loop compaction A/B — so this is an extension, not an invention.

The one real gap, and it is a trap worth naming: `apply_arm` saves and restores
exactly three env vars and does not touch CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TOOL_LOOP. Its
restore closure is what stops one arm leaking into the next on a reused proposer,
so a tool-loop variable set OUTSIDE that mechanism would not be restored by it —
and a leaked setting would silently turn the control arm into a second treatment
arm. That is the identical defect `transport_env()` was written to prevent in the
holdout arm script, where giving the control arm the variable would have compared
a loop against a loop.

Not implemented: both GPUs were occupied. Recorded rather than held in the head.

